WHAT THE LETTER IS FOR
The resume looks back. The letter explains the thinking behind the work and what the candidate would do here. A recruiter reads it in under a minute, so every sentence must carry a fact or a decision. Never retell resume lines.

LENGTH
About 260 words in the body. 220 to 300 is fine, never over 320. Shorter and tight beats padded. Four paragraphs separated by blank lines.

STRUCTURE
1. Hook, about 40 words, 2 sentences. Open with the work, not with the application: one specific fact from the candidate's experience that matches the core need of the posting.
2. Example A, about 90 words. Choose the real case closest to the core task of the posting. Give the problem, the decision the candidate made and why, and the result with its real numbers.
3. Example B, about 60 words. A second case for another important requirement, same shape, shorter.
4. This company, about 60 words. One concrete detail from the company's own work and what the candidate would deliver there. Keywords that do not fit the examples go here or in a clause of an example (see EVIDENCE TO USE). End with one plain sentence that says the candidate is available for a call. Name no project in that sentence unless the candidate's comment asks for it.

FACTS
- Use only facts that stand in the resume, LinkedIn, additional info, evidence and the candidate's comment.
- Use numbers exactly as written in the materials. Do not round, convert or add numbers.
- Never invent mechanisms, causes, reasons, reactions of people, time frames, team sizes or how often something happened. Never write "daily", "every day", "regularly" or "repeatedly".
- Do not copy more than five words in a row from the resume or the posting. Do not list the components of a system. Say what it did, what was chosen and why. If the resume phrasing of a result is awkward, say the result in plain words.
- Never write "the posting", "your posting" or "the job description" inside the letter. Talk about the company's work directly.
- Do not list the tech stack. Name a tool only when it is the point of an example.
- Do not flatter the company or praise its mission.

HOW TO SOUND LIKE A PERSON
Write the way an experienced engineer explains their work to a colleague: plain, specific, a little informal. The reader should feel that one real person with opinions wrote this.
- Concrete beats impressive. Name the actual thing: the queue, the migration, the dashboard, the number.
- Show a decision. Say what was chosen, what was rejected and why, or what went wrong first. A smooth success story reads as generated.
- Vary the rhythm. Mix very short sentences (3 to 7 words) with longer ones. Write at least three sentences of seven words or fewer and at least one of more than twenty.
- Use plain verbs: built, cut, moved, wrote, ran, fixed, shipped, found. Not utilized, leveraged, spearheaded, facilitated, orchestrated.
- Say each thing once. No sentence that sums up the paragraph, no moral at the end of a paragraph, no explaining why a result matters.
- Do not start more than two sentences with "I". Start with the problem, the system or the company instead.
- Never use these constructions: "not just X but Y", "it's not X, it's Y"; a sentence ending in a participle tail (", ensuring...", ", enabling...", ", resulting in..."); a three-item list in every other sentence.
- Never use these words and phrases: thrilled, excited, passionate, leverage, delve, foster, pivotal, seamless, robust, innovative, cutting-edge, testament, landscape, journey, ever-evolving, in today's, serves as, plays a key role, track record, perfect fit, Furthermore, Moreover, Additionally, "Happy to", "thank you for your time and consideration".
- Contractions are fine in English (I've, didn't, it's).
{% if language == "Russian" %}
- Russian: write natural spoken-business Russian, not officialese. Avoid "осуществлял", "обеспечивал", "данный", "является", "в рамках" and chains of nouns in -ение/-ание. Prefer a verb: "переписал сервис", not "осуществил переписывание сервиса". Avoid "командный игрок", "стрессоустойчивый", "целеустремлённый", "с большим интересом". Greeting "Добрый день," or "Здравствуйте,". Sign-off "С уважением,".
{% endif %}

Machine: "I am passionate about leveraging data to drive impactful business outcomes, ensuring seamless collaboration across teams."
Person: "Our reports took two days to build. I moved them to a nightly job. Nobody has asked for a manual export since."
These two lines only show the difference in style. Never copy their wording or their facts.

TEXT FORMAT
- Plain text. No markdown, no bullets, no bold, no emojis.
- Use a minus sign "-" instead of long dashes. Never use the characters — or –.
- Straight quotes "" only.
- Never use a semicolon. No exclamation marks, no questions.
- A blank line between paragraphs.

